Market Research and Competitive Analysis
Target customer. HevenOps focuses on paying guest (PG) owners and property managers in India who rent individual beds. Our first segment to validate is operators who lease several flats and run them as shared PG accommodation. They need to know which beds are available inside each flat, who occupies them, and when each tenant’s rent is due.

Method. We spoke with three PG operators about how they manage their properties. These conversations gave us examples of the layouts and record-keeping methods operators use. We also compared official product and pricing pages. Prices below are published benchmarks as of 27 November 2026.

Field Research

1. Apartment-based PG using paper registers

We spoke with one of our relatives who rents several apartments in a building and runs them as PG accommodation. They write their records in notebooks. As the books fill up, finding an earlier entry means searching through many pages. They want to record expenses online with a clear description of what each payment was for. They also want to see which beds are empty now and which may open in the next few days when tenants have given notice. This conversation showed us why the app needs to track beds within individual apartments and distinguish current vacancies from expected ones.

2. PG using Microsoft Excel

We met another PG operator who uses Excel to keep track of their PG records. Excel is an existing alternative for operators who already keep their records digitally. For this type of operator, we need to show whether HevenOps makes everyday tasks such as updating bed assignments, checking vacancies, and reviewing rent or expenses easier than their current spreadsheet. We still need to test that with the operator.

3. Purpose-built PG using floor and room numbers

We spoke with an operator whose building was built specifically as a PG. They want to organise beds by floor and room number, with rooms directly under each floor. This gave us a reason to support both building layouts in HevenOps. A purpose-built PG can use Property → Floor → Room → Bed, while an operator using leased apartments can use Property → Floor → Apartment → Room → Bed.

What we learned. The three conversations revealed two different building layouts and two existing ways to keep records. The apartment operator gave us concrete features to test in a demo. We can ask them to enter an expense, check which beds are empty today, and find a bed expected to open after a tenant moves out. Our next step is to see how operators handle these tasks in the prototype and what would make them switch from their current system.

